## ELEVATOR PITCHES

Three distinct pitches calibrated for different audiences. Each serves as a north star
reminder of what this project is and who it is for. Updated as characters and details
develop.

### Player-Facing Pitch

Tone: exciting, visceral, personal. Platform: Steam page, social media, trailer narration.

“The sun is dying. The AI that was supposed to protect humanity is hunting you. And the
only thing standing between Earth and extinction is a band.


Dystenic Transmission is a Metroidvania RPG where you play as five musicians fighting
to survive the collapse of a world that was never as safe as it seemed. Switch between
band members on the fly, chain rhythm-based attacks to the pulse of a CyberSludge
soundtrack, and navigate a dying Earth where every choice closes a door behind you
forever.

The band lives and breathes together — or falls apart trying. Lose a member and their
instrument drops from the soundtrack, leaving silence where there used to be
something vital.

This is not a game about saving the world. It’s about learning why it needed saving in
the first place.”

### Developer / Collaborator-Facing Pitch

Tone: visionary, specific, artistically serious. Platform: portfolio, creative briefs, team
recruitment.

“Dystenic Transmission: The Game is a Metroidvania RPG built around a single
rhetorical argument — that blind trust in authority corrodes everything it claims to
protect.

The game follows five musicians through the collapse of Earth’s AI governance system,
a dying sun, and a cosmic horror that has been feeding on civilizations for centuries.
Players control the full band, switching fluidly between characters whose individual
mechanics, narrative specialties, and relationship dynamics shape every aspect of the
experience.

Music is not decoration here — it is the nervous system of every mechanic. Rhythm
drives combat rewards. Musical tone signals consequence. A permadeath system
removes a fallen member’s instrument from the soundtrack permanently, letting grief be
heard rather than just registered.

Built on a souls-lite progression framework with use-based ability growth, a semi-
opaque consequence system, and a world that visually degrades in real time, the game
is designed as functional fiction — an entertaining medium that delivers meaning
through experience rather than exposition.


The full vision spans five albums, five interconnected world maps, and a transmedia
release strategy pairing game, novel, and music. Tier 1 development targets a proof-of-
concept prototype in Godot, with Album 1 as a complete standalone release.

This is a game with something to say. Every system exists to say it.”

### Publisher / Investor-Facing Pitch

Tone: confident, market-aware, opportunity-focused. Platform: pitch deck, industry
meetings.

“Dystenic Transmission: The Game is a Metroidvania RPG for PC targeting the
underserved intersection of narrative-driven action and music-integrated gameplay.

The Metroidvania genre has seen consistent commercial success in the indie space —
Hollow Knight, Ori, Blasphemous, and Dead Cells collectively represent tens of millions
of units sold. Dystenic Transmission differentiates itself through three market-distinct
pillars:

First — a five-character band system where switching between playable characters mid-
combat, mid-exploration, and mid-narrative creates a cooperative experience for solo
players unlike anything currently in the genre.

Second — a rhythm-integrated combat system tied to an original CyberSludge
soundtrack, where timing, music, and mechanical reward are inseparable. The
soundtrack is not licensed — it is wholly original and genre-defining.

Third — a permadeath system where losing a band member permanently removes their
instrument from the soundtrack, creating an emotional consequence mechanic with no
direct competitor in the space.

The project follows a tiered release strategy: Album 1 as a complete standalone game
establishes the IP, with subsequent releases expanding the universe across four
additional albums. A parallel transmedia strategy — original music and a novel series —
builds audience before and between game releases.


The core audience is the narrative-driven indie gamer who finished Hollow Knight’s lore
and wanted more, the rhythm game player who wants depth beyond the beat, and the
player who reads between the lines of the worlds they inhabit.

Dystenic Transmission doesn’t chase trends. It builds one.”

