## WORLD & MAP STRUCTURE

### Albums 1 & 2 — Earth

- One large interconnected world map divided into distinct regions
- Regions open and close based on story progression and choice consequences
- World continuously degrades — Terminal Dusk arc baked into the map
- Vertical layers: underground, surface, Hex-Sphere interior, upper atmosphere
- Five starting locations geographically distinct across the Earth map
- Some returnable, some permanently closed depending on choices
⚠ **FLAG-06:** Earth regional zones best designed after characters are established — starting
locations define the first zones.

#### Map Progression & Lock-out System

- Lock-outs mostly environmental — the world physically changes around the
    player
- Old paths severed by collapse, flooding, fire, Axiom Cleansings, Terminal Dusk
    decay
- New locations open within old areas — same geography becomes a different
    place
- Some buildings collapse entirely, permanently inaccessible
- Entering the Hex-Sphere is a point of no return for previous surface locations
- Each Album transition locks out most or all prior locations
- Albums 3 onward are strictest — leaving Earth is leaving Earth
- Dead Earth as secret ending territory implies a specific late-game return
    mechanism
- Exploration has genuine urgency — thoroughness rewarded before crossing
    thresholds


### Albums 3–5 — Hub & Spoke

- Hub-and-spoke model: colony ship as central hub, explorable planets as spokes
- Each planet a self-contained world map, roughly half the size of the Earth map
- Story begins on one planet; additional planets unlock progressively
- Planets intentionally incomplete on first visit — returning after unlocking
    something elsewhere opens new areas
- No single planet fully explorable without engaging with others
- Planet maps adaptive — story beats and choices cause war-zone degradation or
    restoration
- Alliance locations can be attacked — potential timed events requiring response
- Deploying an inactive band member can help restore damaged areas if not
    beyond repair
- Full exploration rewards achievements, secrets, allies, enemies, and lore

#### The Ship as Hub

- Alliance HUD — ally count, faction relationships, resistance network status
- Location Selector — planet navigation and fast travel
- Collection Display — artifacts, lore, trophies, alien tech
- Repair & Recharge Stations — permanent tool maintenance
- Salvage Station — processing spent gear into resources or components
- Shop — spending experience on items, upgrades, and information
- Wardrobe & Loadout — outfits, gear configuration, augment management
- Jukebox & Stage — music playback, band performance space, morale and
    cohesion mechanic
- Expands and improves as allies join and resources are recovered
- Alliance attacks on the ship are among the most high-stakes events in Albums 3–
    5

### Vantage Points

- Scattered across all maps — Earth and alien worlds alike
- Stumbled upon, unlocked, or secret — finding all on a map is an achievement
- Each offers a vista moment — world rendered at its most expansive and
    atmospheric
- Some carry environmental storytelling — collapsed cities, dying stars, alien
    megastructures
- Certain characters may comment uniquely on specific vistas


## CORE MECHANICS

### 1. The Switch Mechanic

#### Combat Applications

- Mid-combat switching enabled
- 2–5 character combination attacks
- Tool and advanced skill use requiring specific characters

#### World & Exploration

- Character-specific secret observation
- Tool and skill advancement gates discoveries
- NPC interactions subtly character-dependent

#### Strategy Layer

- Inactive characters dispatchable on missions and objectives
- Dispatched characters temporarily unavailable
- Creates resource tension around who to deploy vs. keep present

#### Achievement Design

- Full band switching is the default intended experience
- Forced switches flagged and do not penalize solo achievement runs
- Each character has a unique solo difficulty quotient
- Achievements tied to both auto-switch and accept-the-fall settings
- Achievement for no characters downed on either setting

#### Enemy Affinity Per Character

- Tier 1 — Dominated by this character
- Tier 2 — Slightly overpowered by this character
- Tier 3 — Even match
- Tier 4 — Slightly overpowers this character
- Tier 5 — Dominates this character


### 2. Combat

#### Feel

- Action-forward fluidity balanced with RPG stat and cooldown depth
- 2.5D with dynamic camera — shifts, execution close-ups, screen-shake for
    impacts and environmental events
- Fluid, flowing movement and attack animations as visual priority

#### Rhythm Mechanic

- Timing attacks to the underlying music rewards players
- Music functionally integrated, not just atmospheric
- Tempo changes during boss fights, biome transitions, and intense moments
    serve as gameplay signals
- Rewards: critical hits, stamina conservation, unique character chain rewards,
    escalating with higher chains

#### Resources

- Stamina — primary resource throughout all five albums
- Warmth — introduced late Album 1 / early Album 2, critical post-Hex-Sphere
    crash
- Biome-specific resources — each location has unique resources to manage
- Warmth HUD appears subtly at first, escalating in urgency through Album 2

#### Post-Hex-Sphere Crash — Terminal Dusk Surface

- Stealth system — hiding from feral patrols; detection via sight, sound, and heat
- Cold mitigation as active survival mechanic
- Encumbered movement — injuries, terrain, and cold affect mobility
- Characters visually struggle — labored animations, visible breath
- Combat possible but costly — avoidance encouraged


### 3. Permadeath & Health System

#### Persistent Health Monitoring

- Both active and inactive characters have continuously tracked health
- Inactive members exist in near-background paths — visible, active, interacting
    automatically with background enemies, NPCs, and environmental elements
- Player can offer pause-menu assistance, commands, or macros to inactive
    members without swapping
- Easier difficulties: inactive members largely self-sufficient
- Harder difficulties: active monitoring critical, resource and item management
    urgent

#### Non-Permadeath Mode — Injury System

- Active character at 0 HP: blacks out, dragged to last safe-zone to recover
- Player may switch to another member while downed member heals
- Or continue with injured member: reduced stats, labored movement, worsening
    with accumulated injuries
- Injury state transfers seamlessly between active and inactive
- Healing: slow natural regeneration or healing items; recovery countdown extends
    with difficulty

**Inactive member downed in background:** One other inactive member escorts the
fallen with limited movement and minimal combat until reaching a safe-zone.
**Two inactive members downed:** Remaining two rush both fallen to previous safe-
zone; player receives signal to return.
This gives the genre's respawn mechanic a human, narratively grounded rationale.

#### Permadeath Mode — Band & Solo

- Inactive members permanently die after receiving more than two injuries without
    fully healing a previous injury
- Creates urgent resource tension around healing priority across all five members

⚠ **FLAG-01:** Do healing items fully reset injury accumulation in permadeath mode, or only
restore HP while injury remains on record? Significant impact on difficulty tuning.

**When a member permanently dies — Soundtrack:** That member's instrumental role
removed from the soundtrack, replaced with signal noise and static. Overall sound


becomes unbalanced and unsettling. Music as Language delivering grief directly to the
player's senses.

**When a member permanently dies — Narrative:** Interactions up to their death
preserved as highlights. Remaining members and NPCs discuss the deceased. NPCs
ask about the missing member until told the truth, then discuss them according to
affinity held at time of death.

**When a member permanently dies — World:** Member-specific routes, paths, and
access points locked out. Faction, ally, and NPC interactions tied to that member locked
out. Specialty assistance permanently lost. Exploration, lore, and knowledge discoveries
tied to them inaccessible.

#### Band Permadeath Mode

- Player must exhaust all inactive members before the final active member falls
- Reaching that final fall triggers the Special Bad Ending

#### Solo Permadeath Mode

- Manual switching removed
- Whole band interactions still occur at convergence points — important story
    beats where full band naturally gathers
- Inter-member affinity system active:
    ◦ If affinities drop below Solo Mode threshold, members can abandon the
       player
    ◦ Each departed member makes the path significantly harder
    ◦ Game remains beatable regardless — special limited-use items substitute for
       story-critical member interactions

⚠ **FLAG-14:** Abandoned members (Solo Mode) vs deceased members need distinct narrative
and ending treatment.

#### The Weight of Absence

Across all permadeath modes, loss of a member is felt through:

- Soundtrack degradation — signal noise replacing their instrument
- Visual absence in the near-background
- Lost specialty assistance
- Restricted exploration and discovery


- Reduced faction and NPC interaction depth
- Narrative acknowledgment by surviving members and the world

#### Ending Matrix

**Normal Mode:** Standard Metroidvania forgiveness. Endless attempts. Injuries as
tactical challenge, not permanent consequence. Endings based on choices, signal
state, and narrative branches.
**Band Mode Endings:** Focus on the whole band. Permadeath variables: which
members survived affects ending specifics. Combinations of surviving members
produce distinct ending variations.
**Solo Mode Endings:** Special solo ending for each individual character. Every
combination of surviving and deceased/abandoned members produces distinct ending
variations. Combined with existing ending variables — good, bad, neutral, secret. Most
complex ending matrix, rewarding deep replayability.
⚠ **FLAG-02:** Ending matrix complexity enormous. Hundreds of potential states. Requires
careful branching architecture. Signal Framework carries much of this weight.

### 4. Movement & Traversal

#### Universal Movement

- Running, jumping, basic climbing, limited parkour — human-adjacent
- Grappling — learnable, progressively advanced into throws, combos,
    combination attacks

#### Character Differentiation

- Same universal moves, different stat expression — speed, jump height, climbing
    efficiency, grapple range
- Augments and fractured AI cores enhance within human-adjacent limits

#### Gear & Tools

- Anything beyond human-adjacent ability requires equipment
- Gear is non-permanent — tactical usage and resource management

#### Character Switching During Traversal

- Switching on the ground in appropriate areas only


- Mid-air switching limited to combat aerial attacks
- Players pre-plan which character leads a traversal section — deliberate puzzle
    layer

#### Hacking & Environment Interaction

- Basic interactions available to all characters
- Synth Master is primary hacker — learns and advances progressively
- Map regions gate interaction complexity to match character advancement
⚠ **FLAG-13:** New Game+ mode flagged as potential future feature enabling cross-region
interactions previously gated.

### 5. Progression & Leveling

#### Growth — Souls-lite Framework

- Experience is a spent resource — deliberate investment
- Each character maintains their own experience pool
- Three simultaneous growth tracks: Experience (stats), Story beats (ability
    access), Tool knowledge (discovered)

#### Ability Growth — Use-Based

- Abilities strengthen through practice
- Mastery opens enhancements, advancements, and additional dynamics

#### Character Differentiation

- Unique experience pools, stat weights, and progression structures per role
- Shared baseline actions across all five
- Specialties can alter story progression positively or negatively

#### Band Dispatch System

- Absent characters return with: tool discoveries, survivor count changes, gear
    salvage, resource scouting, route changes, lore knowledge


#### Gear Interaction

- Character growth governs gear effectiveness, efficiency, and maintenance
    capability

#### Narrative Beat → Ability Pipeline

- Story beats open the route to learn → practice develops the ability → mastery
    unlocks enhancements

### 6. Choice & Consequence

#### Consequence Visibility — Semi-Opaque with Musical Signaling

- No explicit good/bad UI messaging
- Subtle musical tones: harmonic resolution (good), missed note (bad), silence
    (neutral), chord (secret)
- Players who listen learn to read the music as a language

#### Tracking

- Faction and relationship levels tracked internally
- Unavailable dialogue options not shown — player sees only what is accessible
- Consequences surface through stats, not explicit reputation meters
- Standard thralls dialogue-accessible based on faction affinity; extra options for
    specific characters

#### Branching Structure

- Significant diverging narrative branches with compounding choices directing
    each open or closed
- Major story beats act as transmission checkpoints evaluating signal state

#### Reversibility

- Some consequences mitigable, some permanently locked in
- Semi-opaque signaling makes permanent locks feel earned


### 7. Threat System

#### Enemy Categories — Albums 1 & 2

- Bots, Flying Drones (go feral post-crash), Rogue Wardens (sub-boss seraph-
    bots)
- Augment-Husks — Star-eater corrupted, feral berserkers, bridge between earthly
    and cosmic threat
- Star-Eaters — revealed end of Album 2, grand-scale final enemies Albums 3–5

#### Behavioral Tiers

**Standard Thralls:** Calculated, composed, coordinated. Adapt and escalate. Organic
thralls retain faction affinity — dialogue possible via character specialty.
**Augment-Husks:** Mentally absent, purely instinctual. Stronger, faster, more erratic.
Uncontrollable by Axioms. Impossible to communicate with. Emerge late Album 2. Final
sequence: overwhelming Husk pursuit to last secure location.

#### Husk Swarm Intelligence

- Under 7 units — limited coordination, infighting, exploitable chaos
- 8+ units — Hive-Swarm state, near-impossible to defeat; shifts to chase or hiding
    mechanics

#### Augment Apostles — Albums 3+

- Originally Star-eater commanded, retain independent thought, can become allies

#### Environmental Threats

- Crumbling structures, fire, acid, freezing spray, electricity, high winds, falling
    debris, Hex-Sphere segments

#### Boss Structure Philosophy

- Each album: mini-bosses, sub-bosses, main bosses, hidden/secret bosses
- Prior choices and paths can make bosses harder or easier; some have special
    mechanics rewarding preparation
⚠ **FLAG-07:** Boss structure details TBD as writing develops.


#### Star-eater Influence Arc

- Albums 1–2: indirect through Axiom failures and corruption
- Late Album 2: Husks emerge, horror escalates
- End of Album 2: Star-eater physically emerges from the Sun
- Albums 3–5: grand scale confrontation across the graveyard and beyond

## HUD & UI/UX PHILOSOPHY

### Overall Philosophy — Minimal & World-First

- Exploration mode: near-empty screen — the world speaks for itself
- Combat mode: minimal overlay, only what is immediately necessary
- Vantage points and cinematic moments suppress HUD entirely
- The game trusts the player to read the world rather than the interface
- HUD itself evolves across the game — visual state reflects narrative progression

### Diegetic Presentation — Default

- HUD elements as in-world augment projections — holographic overlays,
    fractured data readouts, corrupted signal displays
- Health pools as biometric readouts from augment systems
- Stamina as physical exertion indicator — breathing pattern, body heat signature
- Warmth as thermal reading, degrading as environment gets colder
- Rhythm indicator as waveform or signal pulse, not a traditional beat bar
- Narrative signal state subtly present in UI texture — clearer when clear, distorted
    when corrupted

### HUD Degradation

- Earth — Terminal Dusk: HUD deteriorates during encumbered, cold, high-stress
    sequences
- Flickering biometrics, signal dropout, thermal warnings, corrupted warmth
    displays, rhythm waveform harder to read
- Albums 3–5: degradation triggered by specific encounters — Star-eater
    interference, Hive-Swarm proximity, planetary hazards
- Augment Apostle encounters may produce unique alien interference patterns
- Each alien world's technology may interact with the HUD distinctly


### HUD Style Options & Customization

- Diegetic augment display (default), Traditional clean overlay (accessibility), Alien
    technology aesthetic (Albums 3–5 option)
- Preset scales and loadouts: Signal Clear, Distorted, Static — themed to the world
- Unlockable HUD themes through gameplay — cosmetic but meaningful

### Stage & Jukebox

- Player reward space — decompress, enjoy the soundtrack, inhabit the world
- Tracks unlocked through gameplay progression
- Morale and cohesion mechanic — band cohesion as a background stat
- High cohesion boosts combination attacks, rhythm chains, dispatch success
- Low cohesion creates friction — slower chains, reduced combo effectiveness
- Stage rhythm mini-game — 3 difficulty tiers, fully optional, never story-blocking
- Each character plays differently reflecting their instrument and rhythm identity
- Rewards: cosmetics, cohesion boosts, experience, possible lore unlocks

### Menu Design — CyberSludge Visual & Sonic Identity

#### Visual Identity

- Corrupted analog broadcasts — scan lines, signal interference, glitch artifacts
- Heavy textural contrast — raw organic elements bleeding into cold digital
    geometry
- Color palette: deep blacks, sickly industrial greens and ambers (Albums 1–2),
    shifting to colder blues and alien hues (Albums 3–5)
- Typography hand-defaced over a mechanical base — meaning scratched into a
    corporate interface
- UI elements worn, used, slightly broken — degraded infrastructure, not clean
    futurism

#### Sonic Identity

- Ambient CyberSludge — heavier tracks reserved for combat and key moments
- Menu ambience: corrupted signal noise with buried melodic fragments
- Navigation sounds: short degraded synth tones or muffled percussive hits


- Transition sounds: brief analog distortion bursts

#### The 25 CyberSludge Tracks

- 6 tracks — Album 1 related
- 12 tracks — Album 2 (Axiom of the Black Sun) — most musically and
    mechanically developed
- 1 track each — Albums 3, 4, 5
- 3 intercultural experiments — Japanese, Egyptian, Hindu instrumentation
    merged with CyberSludge
- 1 full narrative summary track
- Album 2 Track 7 onward increasingly self-written; Tracks 9–12 fully written by the
    creator

#### Audio Production Assets

- Stems from Suno productions available for reconstruction and personalization in
    DAW
- Various DAWs, sequencers, audio packs, loops, and instruments available
- Plan: reconstruct and personally adjust stems to establish a fully owned
    CyberSludge sonic identity
- Strong audio production capability is a significant advantage over most solo
    developers

## DESIGN PRINCIPLES

- Everything established is a guiding principle — malleable and adaptive as the
    project grows
- The band is the core experience — solo runs are the challenge, not the default
- Music is mechanically integrated throughout — rhythm, consequence signaling,
    tone, identity, morale, grief
- The world itself tells the story — visual progression, environmental decay, HUD
    evolution
- Player agency is paramount — the game informs without dictating
- Narrative and mechanics are unified — every system serves the story and the
    rhetorical argument
- The game is a band experience — solo runs are the equivalent of a guitarist
    performing the whole album alone
- Exploration has genuine urgency — the world closes behind you as it dies


- The music is not background — it is a language the game speaks to the player
- Loss is felt, not just registered — absence reshapes the world, the sound, and
    the story

